thread:
Alex Nguyen: Quick design question: for multi-part generation requests (batch of prompts in one call) should we try to map partial failures to per-item HTTP codes (207?) or keep HTTP 200 and include per-item status fields in the body? Concern is client lib complexity vs backwards compatibility.

Rosa Diaz: Strong preference for single HTTP code. 207 is weird and some infra (LBs, monitoring) don't play nice. Return 200 + items: [{id, status, result, error_code}] is simplest. Add a top-level header for quick filtering e.g. `x-redwood-multi-status: partial` or `ok`.

Samir Patel: Agree 200+body. Important callout: when any item fails due to quota we MUST return per-item error_code=quota_exceeded and emit a top-level `x-redwood-quota-hit: true` header so proxy/edge can short-circuit downstream billing logic. 429 stays for single-prompt calls that are entirely rejected.

Claire Li: SDK ergonomics: provide helpers to normalize both single and multi responses. Example snippet: ```js
// sdk normalize
function normalize(resp){
  if (Array.isArray(resp.body.items)){
    return resp.body.items.map(i => ({
      ok: i.status === 'ok',
      error: i.error_code || null,
      text: i.result || null,
      degraded: !!i.degraded
    }));
  }
  return [{ ok: resp.status === 200, text: resp.body?.text }];
}
```

Alex Nguyen: Nice. Also: telemetry and console UX. We need per-route histograms for "partial failures" and a breakdown by reason (quota/capacity/model_crash). Console should allow filtering by item-level error_code.

Rosa Diaz: For rollout UX: when we roll a new model variant, we'll show an "item-level mismatch" badge if the fallback rate >2% for last 30m. Also surface small diffs in the traces view (first 3 tokens) to help debug regressions quickly.

Samir Patel: Telemetry fields proposal: `multi_status_overall` (ok|partial|failed), then `item_fail_reason` tag (quota|timeout|model_error|auth). For sampling, attach `sample_degraded=true` and follow PII redaction rules. Keep rate of full pairs sampled <=0.5% to limit costs.

Claire Li: Client retry guidance: recommend exponential backoff with full jitter; if `x-redwood-quota-hit=true` or any item has `error_code=quota_exceeded`, client should not retry immediately but surface a clear Error type (QuotaError) to caller. We should document this in the SDK reference. Also add header `x-redwood-retry-suggest: 30` when we want clients to wait longer.

Alex Nguyen: One more API flag idea to support advanced clients: `Preference: accept-item-errors-as-details` header. When present, we always return 200+items even if all items failed; otherwise, if all failed we fall back to 4xx/5xx as today. This helps diagnostic clients avoid parsing exceptions. Thoughts?

Rosa Diaz: I like that opt-in header. Make it opt-in only (so current clients keep behavior). Add note: don't expose raw model traces unless `consent=full-trace` in dev mode.

Samir Patel: Action items: I'll add telemetry fields to the schema (include `item_fail_reason` and `multi_status_overall`), Claire can add SDK normalize helpers and error classes, Rosa will prototype console filters and the rollout badge, Alex will draft the small RFC for the opt-in Preference header and sample size limits.

